From a85ce775e26f24168859f3423abca72fcea3f99a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 08:00:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: change the monitor while Kodi runs, or offer
 to restart

A change of the Monitor setting was used from the next start of Kodi only, and
a notification said so: to change from the TV to a monitor on the same card the
user had to start Kodi again, and to know that, read the notification.

Now the setting is taken where a start of Kodi would take it (MoveFor() of
DRMOutputChoice.h, with every connected display counting as one that can be
used, as at a start):

- another output of the graphics device in use: Kodi moves to it at once, and
  Kodi's own question to keep the change follows (13110/13111, 10 s). When it is
  not answered yes, Kodi goes back to the output it was on;
- an output of another graphics device: the user is asked whether to start Kodi
  again now (yes: it is started again, and the start takes that output; no: the
  setting names it from the next start). What Kodi draws with lives in the
  device in use: its EGL display and OpenGL context, the GBM surface and its
  buffers, every texture of the interface, the decoders that hand their
  pictures to it. Kodi has no way to make all of them anew on another device
  while it runs;
- the output in use (named, or Default where a start gives it): nothing is done
  and nothing is asked. Before, the mode of the output was set again and the
  question to keep it was shown for nothing.

A move within the device is made from the thread that draws, while nothing
plays and the output carries the interface only (no plane of a video, no HDR
signalling, no change of mode that waits or that a video that opens told of,
nothing of native Dolby Vision, no composition over HDR, no output that a flip
which did not come turned off): else the setting keeps what it had and a
notification says to stop playback. A change made from elsewhere (a remote control of Kodi)
or while the display of Kodi sleeps is used from the next start, as before.

How it moves, fail-closed at each step:

- the output is read again with its display probed (what Kodi knew of it may
  be of when it started), and given the mode that its display prefers, the CRTC
  in use where its encoders can drive it (MoveCrtc(): the plane that shows Kodi
  stays then), else another one with a plane that takes what Kodi shows;
- the driver is asked first, with a buffer of the size of the window there, in
  a commit that only asks (TestOutputMove()): where it would not take it,
  nothing changes, and the user is asked whether to start Kodi again (a start
  turns on every display that is connected);
- what Kodi knows of its output becomes what it knows of the new one, as at a
  start (the information of the display for HDR, Dolby Vision and 3D, the HDR
  choices of its driver, the colorimetry and the HDR signalling of the
  interface on it; a failure of native output on the display before counts no
  more); the resolutions are those of the new output, and the one of the
  settings among them is set, as at a start (GetDisplayResolution());
- the commit of that mode makes the move: the output before leaves its CRTC,
  every other output of the device goes off, and a refusal falls back to
  nothing (the request before it is of the other output, in another size).
  Where it is refused the screen still shows what it showed, and Kodi goes back
  to that output the same way. Only where that is refused too is Kodi started
  again, which shows it as a start chooses.

The settings list the outputs of the device in use as the kernel knows them
now (without a probe), from the thread that draws and while no video plays: a
display connected since Kodi started is offered, and can be moved to. The
display watch follows the output in use after a move. The end of Kodi gives
the CRTC back to the output Kodi started on, with its mode, also after a move.
The help of the setting says what it does (#39853, in place of Kodi's #40803).

Host test of DRMOutputChoice.h: where a change takes Kodi on the user's PC
(three displays on one card), and in 24000 cases of three outputs on two
devices the same verdict of whether Kodi moves as before, a move "here" always
on the device in use; the CRTC of a move; 11 mutations of the header caught.
Nothing of the move has run on a graphics card yet.
